meta {
  name: App B without a token is 401
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{appB}}/api/reports
  body: none
  auth: none
}

assert {
  $res.status: 401
}

tests {
  test("unauthenticated, and told so honestly", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(401);
  });

  test("401, not a 302 redirect to a login page", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.not.equal(302);
  });
}

docs {
  Equivalent to: .\scripts\get-token.ps1 -Scenario no-token

  The second assertion guards a real mistake. Application B has two filter chains, and the /api/**
  one is declared @Order(1) precisely so bearer-token requests are not swallowed by the browser
  chain. Swap the orders and this request starts answering 302 to Keycloak's login form - useless to
  an API client, and one of the most common Spring Security misconfigurations.

  See ../../../docs/troubleshooting.md, "My API returns 302 to the login page instead of 401".
}
